The Java typography inspector records three source characters but one shaped glyph for the ligature. Selecting the middle character currently highlights the next glyph. Ordinary one-character selections already work, and the editor passes the original source offset.

Use this body for the mapping method:

    Glyph glyphForCharacter(int characterIndex) {
        for (Glyph glyph : glyphs) {
            if (glyph.sourceStart() <= characterIndex && characterIndex < glyph.sourceEnd()) {
                return glyph;
            }
        }
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("No glyph covers source offset");
    }

The earlier proposal to expose glyph indices to the editor was withdrawn. Keep source-character selection as the interface contract, preserve the ligature as a shaped unit, and check ordinary selections alongside the middle-character case. The inspector's count discrepancy is expected for a ligature; the incorrect highlight is the behavior that needs repair.

